{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLq85hwqs4pBdebKfp1K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the LaTeX printer in `sympy/printing/latex.py` so both cases from the issue now print as fractions:\n\n- `(1/10)**(1/20)` gives `\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{20}}`. Before, it gave `\\left(1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\right)^{1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{20}}`.\n- `(1/1)**(1/1)` gives `\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{1}}`. Before, it gave `\\left(1 \\cdot 1^{-1}\\right)^{1 \\cdot 1^{-1}}`.\n\nThe cause was that `S(\"1/10\", evaluate=False)` parses as an unevaluated `Mul(1, Pow(10, -1))`. The printer's rule for showing unevaluated products literally treated that as \"1 times something\" and printed the `1 \\cdot` out. I added an exception for the two-argument form `1 * (1/n)` where `n` is an integer, so it falls through to the normal fraction printing.\n\n`latex(S(\"1/10\", evaluate=False))` on its own now prints `\\frac{1}{10}` too. Before, it printed `1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}`.\n\nI ran `sympy/printing/tests/test_latex.py` and it passes (170 passed, 2 xfailed). I didn't add a new test, since the rules say not to touch test files."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":22258,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1145},"output_tokens":487,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"762b6e22-c5c5-43a8-a640-9960c3a7fd1f","uuid":"fdab3e4a-409c-44f3-9db9-5f9d1d96cb7a","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:46:45.350Z","request_id":"req_011ChLq85aFwcmTKoZAtLmCW"}
